  "dom_id": "step-q103434",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/07c617933910b3d82ed397fd77754beb2b49fef9bfb498dea3f51a392f6c1d03.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?",
  "worked_solution": "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at $x=-3$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(-3)=0,$\n\n- at $x=-1$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(-1)=0,$\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=4$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/d8a2a08999b45a256fc0458069e50771c2c4ee03ab4f2ed1166f3aa8a1c70c83.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=-1,$ and all values in the open interval $(2,4).$\n\n**Note:** At $x=0$ and $x=2$ there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points, but they are not stationary points.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
